{
  "lesson_summary": "Today's lesson plans to teach subtraction of up to 4-digit numbers, both with and without regrouping, using the written column method. The lesson will demonstrate the process with the example of 3,600 − 47, focusing on how regrouping renames one larger unit into ten smaller ones without altering the total value.",
  "lesson_summary_short": "Today's lesson plans to teach subtraction of up to 4-digit numbers, using the example 3,600 − 47.",
  "checks_summary": "This quiz checks whether the class can subtract 4-digit numbers with and without regrouping, understanding the concept of renaming.",
  "questions": [
    {
      "slo_id": "S1",
      "level": "recall",
      "question": "Which of these is the correct way to write a subtraction problem in columns?",
      "options": [
        "Ones under ones, tens under tens",
        "Numbers written from left to right",
        "Any way you like"
      ],
      "correct_index": 0,
      "explanation": "In column subtraction, digits must be aligned by their place value: ones under ones, tens under tens, and so on. This ensures you subtract the correct values from each other.",
      "selected_because": "The lesson ended on 'Digits stay in their own columns: ones with ones, tens with tens.'",
      "distractor_misconceptions": {
        "1": "Believes order of digits does not matter in column subtraction.",
        "2": "Thinks column alignment is not important for subtraction."
      },
      "option_feedback": {
        "correct": "That's right! Digits must always stay in their own columns: ones with ones, tens with tens, hundreds with hundreds, and thousands with thousands.",
        "wrong": {
          "1": "If you write numbers from left to right without aligning columns, you might subtract a ten from a hundred, which is not correct. Always align ones under ones, tens under tens, and so on.",
          "2": "The way you write the numbers in subtraction is very important. To get the correct answer, you must always align digits by their place value, like ones under ones."
        }
      },
      "figure": null,
      "figure_role": null
    },
    {
      "slo_id": "S1",
      "level": "apply",
      "question": "Subtract: $\\begin{array}{rr} & 8365 \\\\ - & 55 \\\\ \\hline & \\end{array}$",
      "options": [
        "8310",
        "8315",
        "8420"
      ],
      "correct_index": 1,
      "explanation": "Subtracting 5 from 5 in the ones column gives 0. Subtracting 5 from 6 in the tens column gives 1. The hundreds and thousands columns remain unchanged, resulting in 8,310.",
      "selected_because": "Class practised on their own: 'Solve 8,365 − 55 in Th-H-T-O columns.'",
      "distractor_misconceptions": {
        "0": "Made a mistake in the tens column, possibly subtracting 6 from 5.",
        "2": "Added the numbers instead of subtracting them."
      },
      "option_feedback": {
        "correct": "Excellent! When you subtract 5 from 5 in the ones place, you get 0. Then, 6 minus 5 in the tens place is 1. The hundreds and thousands places remain the same, giving you 8,310.",
        "wrong": {
          "0": "Check your subtraction in the tens column. Remember, you subtract 5 from 6, not 6 from 5. The correct answer in the tens place is 1.",
          "2": "It looks like you added the numbers instead of subtracting them. Remember the minus sign tells you to take away."
        }
      },
      "figure": null,
      "figure_role": null
    },
    {
      "slo_id": "S2",
      "level": "understand",
      "question": "Look at the picture. Which column needs regrouping to subtract?",
      "options": [
        "Ones column",
        "Tens column",
        "Hundreds column"
      ],
      "correct_index": 0,
      "explanation": "In the ones column, you need to subtract 7 from 0. Since 0 is smaller than 7, you cannot subtract directly and need to regroup from the tens column.",
      "selected_because": "The worked example 3,600 - 47 starts with regrouping in the ones column.",
      "distractor_misconceptions": {
        "1": "Confuses the need for regrouping in the ones column with the tens column.",
        "2": "Incorrectly identifies the hundreds column as needing regrouping first."
      },
      "option_feedback": {
        "correct": "You got it! In the ones column, we have 0 and need to subtract 7. Since 0 is smaller than 7, we must regroup from the tens column first.",
        "wrong": {
          "1": "Look closely at the ones column first. Can you subtract 7 from 0? No, so that's where we need to regroup. The tens column will be affected by this regrouping, but the ones column is where the problem starts.",
          "2": "The hundreds column has 6, and we are subtracting 0 from it. That can be done directly. The problem starts in the ones column, where we need to subtract 7 from 0."
        }
      },
      "figure": {
        "type": "base_ten",
        "thousands": 3,
        "hundreds": 6,
        "tens": 0,
        "ones": 0
      },
      "figure_role": "read_off"
    },
    {
      "slo_id": "S2",
      "level": "apply",
      "question": "After regrouping 1 hundred into 10 tens in the problem 3,600 − 47, how many hundreds are left?",
      "options": [
        "5 hundreds",
        "6 hundreds",
        "10 hundreds"
      ],
      "correct_index": 0,
      "explanation": "When you regroup 1 hundred from 6 hundreds, you are left with 5 hundreds. The 1 hundred becomes 10 tens.",
      "selected_because": "The worked example shows '6 H → 5 H, 10 T'.",
      "distractor_misconceptions": {
        "1": "Forgets to subtract the regrouped hundred.",
        "2": "Confuses the regrouped tens with the hundreds column."
      },
      "option_feedback": {
        "correct": "That's correct! When you regroup 1 hundred from 6 hundreds, you are left with 5 hundreds. The 1 hundred is then used to make 10 tens.",
        "wrong": {
          "1": "Remember, when you take 1 hundred to regroup it, you must subtract it from the original number of hundreds. So, 6 hundreds become 5 hundreds.",
          "2": "The 10 tens are what you get when you regroup 1 hundred. But the question asks how many hundreds are left in the hundreds column after you regroup."
        }
      },
      "figure": null,
      "figure_role": null
    },
    {
      "slo_id": "S2",
      "level": "understand",
      "question": "Why do we regroup in subtraction?",
      "options": [
        "To make the top digit larger than the bottom digit in a column",
        "To change the total value of the number",
        "To make the problem easier to write"
      ],
      "correct_index": 0,
      "explanation": "Regrouping is done when the top digit in a column is smaller than the bottom digit, making direct subtraction impossible. It allows us to borrow from a larger place value to make the current digit large enough to subtract.",
      "selected_because": "The key fact the lesson turns on: 'Digits stay in their Th-H-T-O columns; regrouping renames 1 larger unit as 10 smaller ones without changing the total.'",
      "distractor_misconceptions": {
        "1": "Believes regrouping alters the overall value of the number.",
        "2": "Thinks regrouping is for presentation, not mathematical necessity."
      },
      "option_feedback": {
        "correct": "Exactly! We regroup when the top digit is too small to subtract from. We take one from the next column and rename it as ten of the current column's units, so we can subtract.",
        "wrong": {
          "1": "Regrouping never changes the total value of the number. It only renames one larger unit as ten smaller ones, like changing 1 hundred into 10 tens. The value stays the same.",
          "2": "Regrouping is not just about making the problem look easier. It's a mathematical step needed to perform subtraction when the top digit is smaller than the bottom digit in a column."
        }
      },
      "figure": null,
      "figure_role": null
    },
    {
      "slo_id": "S2",
      "level": "apply",
      "question": "What is the result of 10 tens after regrouping 1 hundred and then taking 1 ten to make 10 ones in the example 3,600 − 47?",
      "options": [
        "9 tens",
        "10 tens",
        "0 tens"
      ],
      "correct_index": 0,
      "explanation": "First, 1 hundred is regrouped into 10 tens. Then, 1 of those tens is taken to make 10 ones, leaving 9 tens.",
      "selected_because": "The worked example shows '10 T → 9 T, 10 ones'.",
      "distractor_misconceptions": {
        "1": "Forgets to subtract the ten that was regrouped to the ones column.",
        "2": "Incorrectly assumes all tens are used up or none are left."
      },
      "option_feedback": {
        "correct": "That's right! You start with 10 tens from the regrouped hundred. Then, you take 1 of those tens to give to the ones column, leaving you with 9 tens.",
        "wrong": {
          "1": "Remember, after you get 10 tens from the hundred, you still need to take 1 of those tens to give to the ones column. So, you won't have all 10 tens left.",
          "2": "You start with 10 tens after regrouping the hundred. You only give 1 of those tens to the ones column, so you still have tens left, not 0."
        }
      },
      "figure": null,
      "figure_role": null
    },
    {
      "slo_id": "S2",
      "level": "apply",
      "question": "Subtract: $\\begin{array}{rr} & 4235 \\\\ - & 9 \\\\ \\hline & \\end{array}$",
      "options": [
        "4226",
        "4236",
        "4244"
      ],
      "correct_index": 0,
      "explanation": "To subtract 9 from 5 in the ones column, you need to regroup 1 ten from 3 tens, making it 2 tens and 15 ones. Then, 15 - 9 = 6. The tens column becomes 2, and the hundreds and thousands columns remain unchanged.",
      "selected_because": "Class practised on their own: 'Solve 4,235 − 9 in Th-H-T-O columns.'",
      "distractor_misconceptions": {
        "1": "Made a mistake in regrouping or subtracting in the ones column, possibly adding 9 to 5.",
        "2": "Added the numbers instead of subtracting, or made a mistake in the tens column."
      },
      "option_feedback": {
        "correct": "Great job! You regrouped 1 ten to make 15 ones, then 15 minus 9 is 6. The tens column became 2, and the rest stayed the same, giving you 4,226.",
        "wrong": {
          "1": "Check your subtraction in the ones column. You need to regroup from the tens place to make 15 ones, then subtract 9 from 15. The answer is not 6 if you just subtract 9 from 5.",
          "2": "It looks like you might have added the numbers or made a mistake in the tens column after regrouping. Remember to subtract, and the tens column will have 2 after regrouping."
        }
      },
      "figure": null,
      "figure_role": null
    },
    {
      "slo_id": "S1",
      "level": "understand",
      "question": "Which of these shows the 'ones' column in a subtraction problem?",
      "options": [
        "The rightmost column of digits",
        "The leftmost column of digits",
        "The middle column of digits"
      ],
      "correct_index": 0,
      "explanation": "The ones column is always the rightmost column in a number, representing single units.",
      "selected_because": "The lesson emphasized column alignment and place values (Ones, Tens, H, Th).",
      "distractor_misconceptions": {
        "1": "Confuses the ones column with the thousands column.",
        "2": "Believes the ones column is in the middle, not at the end."
      },
      "option_feedback": {
        "correct": "That's correct! The 'ones' column is always the rightmost column of digits in a number, where we count single units.",
        "wrong": {
          "1": "The leftmost column represents the largest place value, like thousands. The 'ones' column is always on the far right.",
          "2": "The middle column could be tens or hundreds, depending on how many digits the number has. The 'ones' column is always the very last column on the right."
        }
      },
      "figure": {
        "type": "base_ten",
        "hundreds": 1,
        "tens": 2,
        "ones": 3
      },
      "figure_role": "read_off"
    }
  ]
}
